### What is IntStream.rangeClosed?

`IntStream.rangeClosed(start, end)` creates a stream of primitive `int` values from `start` to `end`, with `end` included. It is the natural way to count over an inclusive interval.

```java
package com.bank.account;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class RangeClosedBasics {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 5)
                .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
1
2
3
4
5
```

### How do you generate a sequence of account numbers?

Map each number in the range to an account-number String with `mapToObj`. Here the bank issues ten accounts with a fixed prefix.

```java
package com.bank.account;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class AccountNumberGenerator {
    public List<String> generate(int count) {
        return IntStream.rangeClosed(1, count)
                .mapToObj(i -> String.format("ACC-%05d", i))
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new AccountNumberGenerator().generate(5)
                .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
ACC-00001
ACC-00002
ACC-00003
ACC-00004
ACC-00005
```

The `%05d` format zero-pads each number to five digits for a tidy account code.

### What is the difference between range and rangeClosed?

`range` excludes the upper bound; `rangeClosed` includes it. This single difference decides which one you use.

| Method | Produces | Upper bound | Count | Best for |
|--------|----------|-------------|-------|----------|
| `range(1, 5)` | 1, 2, 3, 4 | excluded | 4 | list indices, counts |
| `rangeClosed(1, 5)` | 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 | included | 5 | real value bounds like 1 to 100 |

```java
package com.bank.account;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class RangeVsRangeClosed {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] exclusive = IntStream.range(1, 5).toArray();
        int[] inclusive = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 5).toArray();
        System.out.println("range(1,5)        = " + Arrays.toString(exclusive));
        System.out.println("rangeClosed(1,5)  = " + Arrays.toString(inclusive));
    }
}
```

```text
range(1,5)        = [1, 2, 3, 4]
rangeClosed(1,5)  = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

### What does mapToObj do?

`IntStream` carries primitive ints, so you cannot directly build objects from it. `mapToObj` converts each `int` into an object, changing the stream type from `IntStream` to `Stream<T>`.

```java
package com.bank.account;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class MapToObjDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // int -> String (object), so the result is Stream<String>
        List<String> labels = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 3)
                .mapToObj(i -> "Branch #" + i)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println(labels);
    }
}
```

```text
[Branch #1, Branch #2, Branch #3]
```

Without `mapToObj` you would be stuck with primitive ints and could not collect into a `List<String>`.

### How do you iterate a list by index?

Use `IntStream.range(0, list.size())`, which yields valid indices `0` through `size - 1`. You can then read `list.get(i)` and pair each element with its position. Here `range` is correct, not `rangeClosed`, because index `size` would be out of bounds.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class IndexIteration {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> employees = List.of("Rajesh", "Priya", "Pankaj");

        List<String> seated = IntStream.range(0, employees.size())
                .mapToObj(i -> (i + 1) + ". " + employees.get(i))
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        seated.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
1. Rajesh
2. Priya
3. Pankaj
```

### What are common mistakes with range and rangeClosed?

The classic bug is using `rangeClosed` for list indices, which reaches `size` and throws `IndexOutOfBoundsException`.

```java
// WRONG: rangeClosed reaches index == size, which is out of bounds
List<String> employees = List.of("Rajesh", "Priya", "Pankaj");
IntStream.rangeClosed(0, employees.size())   // 0,1,2,3 -> index 3 fails
        .mapToObj(employees::get)
        .forEach(System.out::println);
```

```java
// CORRECT: range stops at size - 1, which is the last valid index
IntStream.range(0, employees.size())         // 0,1,2
        .mapToObj(employees::get)
        .forEach(System.out::println);
```

The wrong version throws on the final iteration. The correct version stops at the last valid index.

### What exception can index iteration throw?

An off-by-one error throws `IndexOutOfBoundsException`. The code below shows the exception and then prevents it.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class IndexException {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> employees = List.of("Rajesh", "Priya");
        try {
            IntStream.rangeClosed(0, employees.size())  // reaches index 2
                    .forEach(i -> System.out.println(employees.get(i)));
        } catch (IndexOutOfBoundsException ex) {
            System.out.println("Caught: " + ex.getMessage());
        }

        // Prevention: use range, never rangeClosed, for indices
        IntStream.range(0, employees.size())
                .forEach(i -> System.out.println("ok: " + employees.get(i)));
    }
}
```

```text
Rajesh
Priya
Caught: Index 2 out of bounds for length 2
ok: Rajesh
ok: Priya
```

### Why use IntStream instead of Stream<Integer>?

`IntStream` holds primitive ints, so it avoids boxing each value into an `Integer` object. For numeric ranges and calculations this is faster and uses less memory, and it offers numeric terminal operations like `sum`, `average`, and `max` directly.

```java
package com.bank.account;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class NoBoxing {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // IntStream gives sum() directly, no boxing to Integer
        int total = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 100).sum();
        System.out.println("sum 1..100 = " + total);

        double avg = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 10).average().orElse(0);
        System.out.println("average 1..10 = " + avg);
    }
}
```

```text
sum 1..100 = 5050
average 1..10 = 5.5
```

### When is index-based iteration appropriate?

Use index iteration when you genuinely need the position, not just the element: numbering rows, pairing an element with its index, or stepping through two parallel lists together. When you only need the elements, a plain `for-each` or `list.stream()` is clearer.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class ParallelLists {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> names = List.of("Rajesh", "Priya");
        List<BigDecimal> salaries = List.of(
                new BigDecimal("120000"), new BigDecimal("95000"));

        // Index lets us read matching entries from two lists at once.
        IntStream.range(0, names.size())
                .mapToObj(i -> names.get(i) + " earns " + salaries.get(i))
                .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
Rajesh earns 120000
Priya earns 95000
```

### When should you avoid index iteration?

Avoid it when the position is irrelevant. Reaching for indices to just read elements is more error-prone and less readable than a direct stream.

```java
// AVOID: index iteration when you only need the elements
List<String> names = List.of("Rajesh", "Priya");
IntStream.range(0, names.size())
        .mapToObj(names::get)
        .forEach(System.out::println);
```

```java
// PREFER: iterate elements directly when position does not matter
names.forEach(System.out::println);
// or
names.stream().forEach(System.out::println);
```

### How do boxed and unboxed conversions work?

You can convert between `IntStream` and `Stream<Integer>` with `boxed()` and `mapToInt`. Use this when an API needs boxed `Integer` objects.

```java
package com.bank.account;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class BoxingConversion {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // IntStream -> Stream<Integer> via boxed()
        List<Integer> ids = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 3)
                .boxed()
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println(ids);

        // Stream<Integer> -> IntStream via mapToInt for sum()
        int total = ids.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();
        System.out.println("sum = " + total);
    }
}
```

```text
[1, 2, 3]
sum = 6
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers ask you to state the one-element difference between `range` and `rangeClosed`, show why `rangeClosed(0, size)` breaks list iteration, explain what `mapToObj` returns, justify `IntStream` over `Stream<Integer>` on boxing grounds, and say when index iteration is worth it. A crisp answer: `range` is exclusive and perfect for indices, `rangeClosed` is inclusive and perfect for real value bounds, `mapToObj` turns ints into objects, and you use index iteration only when the position itself matters.
